note #1: all xmm0-31, ymm0-31, zmm0-31 registers are fully supported.

Note #2: Aasm v3.0.0 may still generate invalid machine code when
{sae} or {*-sae} is used with an instruction/register form that does
not support SAE for that vector length.

For example:

    vaddps xmm31, xmm30, xmm30{rd-sae}

may be disassembled by objdump as:

    vaddps zmm31, zmm30, zmm30{rd-sae}

AVX/AVX-512 encoding is extremely complex. Making the encoder fully
accurate, including validation of all invalid operand/decorator
combinations, is a very large task. Even mature assemblers such as
NASM and FASM have historically had invalid or questionable encodings
for some unusual AVX-512 combinations.

For example, NASM accepts:

    vcvtps2ph [rax+rcx], zmm2, {sae}, 10

but objdump may decode the resulting bytes as:

    vcvtps2ph [rax+rcx*1]{bad}{k2}, xmm2, 0xa

This is an invalid combination, as SAE is not supported for this memory form...

-------------------------------------

Another example, FASM accepts:
    
    vcvtps2ph [rax+64]{k4}{z}, zmm29, 0xff

but objdump may decode the resulting bytes as:

    vcvtps2ph YMMWORD PTR [rax+0x40]{k4}{z}/(bad),zmm29,0xff

Because memory-dest can not accept zero mask...